COMMUNITY REIMPLEMENTATIONS

Much of this material is tagged `Live`, meaning it was run and observed rather
than only read. That was possible because of community reverse-engineered
reimplementations, built and maintained without Microware's source:

  os9exec — Lukas Zeller and Beat Forster, and later contributors
  NitrOS-9 — the NitrOS-9 project
  XRoar — the XRoar project

These are credited with a caveat that protects them as much as the reader: a
`Live` claim is evidence about a reimplementation, never about OS-9 itself.
Where a runtime and a Microware manual disagree, this repository treats the
manual as the specification and the runtime as the candidate defect. Both
projects have had real errors found this way, which is a compliment to their
openness rather than a criticism of their quality.


PRESERVATION ARCHIVES

The documentation for this era was never centralised and survives because
people chose to keep it: scanning manuals, correcting OCR, and hosting the
results for decades without being asked to. Specific archives are named in
`SOURCES.md` where a file came from one. The rest of this work rests on that
effort.


